viv: Heads-up: our iOS sample app sees a burst of 429s right after STS refresh when the background paginator is enabled. Happens on warm start and under CI load. Anyone run into mobile-specific quirks?

noah: which SDK and paging config?

viv: swift-sdk v0.3.0, cursor paging, prefetchAhead=20, maxConcurrent=6. STS rotates every 45s in our test. After refresh we get ~10-30s of 429s.

platform-bot: sample headers captured: x-ratelimit-limit:120, x-ratelimit-remaining:0, x-ratelimit-reset:2638881400, x-client-session:swift:cid_9

noah: likely the same root cause as server session attribution flipping when token changes. Mobile has extra complication: NSURLSession sometimes creates new sockets when auth header changes. If prefetch resumes immediately you can generate a burst of "new" sessions.

viv: here's the prefetcher snippet from sampleapp:

```swift
func prefetchLoop() {
  while inflight < maxConcurrent && hasMore {
    inflight += 1
    client.fetchPage(cursor: cur) { _ in inflight -= 1 }
  }
}

auth.onRefresh = { token in
  client.setToken(token)
  // we didn't pause prefetch here
}
```

noah: quick fixes: 1) pause prefetch for a short cool-down after auth change; 2) reuse the same URLSession across token swaps; 3) cancel or mark in-flight requests so they don't retry in parallel with resumed fetches. Add jitter on resume.

dx: added a note to mobile onboarding checklist: "when using short-lived creds + prefetching: reuse URLSession, pause prefetch on rotate, cancel inflight where possible, add randomized resume delay". Will author a tiny sample PR.

viv: implemented pause=1.5s + random resume 100-700ms + URLSession reuse; 429s gone in my local stress runs. Will push repro to the ticket.

mobile-team: thanks, this is super helpful. can we add an explicit example in the swift sampleapp repo showing how to keep URLSession alive across token changes?

dx: yes, will include a code snippet and link to the ticket. Opening PR #4810.
